  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1201 AM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nAnother Heat Advisory in effect for today. Heat indices up to 114 \nare forecast across many locations this afternoon. Thus, a Heat \nAdvisory has been issued from Noon to 7 pm for most of S TX. Major \nto extreme risk of heat related impacts will continue through the \nmiddle of the week and Heat Advisories may be needed at times. By \nthe latter half of the week, models show slightly cooler max temps \ndue to increased rain chances, but still running above normal.\n\nSlightly drier conditions in the mid levels and PWATs generally less \nthan 2 inches today will keep rain chances low to very low across \nmost of S TX, except for the Victoria Crossroads and northern \nCoastal Waters where slightly deeper moisture will bring a 20-50 \npercent chance of showers and thunderstorms. Can not rule out a \nrogue shower or thunderstorm or two along the afternoon sea breeze \nacross the Brush Country as this has been occurring almost daily. \nModels indicate that PWATs are even lower on Tuesday across all of S \nTX which will result in virtually little to no rain chances. Models \nhave been consistent with a surge of deeper moisture advecting back \nacross S TX Wednesday with increasing rain chances through Thursday. \nPWATs are progged to increase to as much as 2.4 inches, which will \nbe conducive for locally heavy rain creating localized flooding \nproblems, especially if the storms are slow moving. Highest rain \nchances Thursday are expected across the eastern half of S TX with \nPoPs from 35-60% and <35% closer to the Rio Grande. \n\nRain chances for Friday and into the weekend are still not clear cut \ndue to differences in the model outputs. The GFS continues to \nindicate drier air moving into S TX as the center of the high moves \ndirectly over S TX, while the Canadian and ECMWF show the center of \nthe high shifting farther west, which will allow for deeper moisture \nto remain over S TX. For now, the low to medium chance across all of \nS TX on Friday looks reasonable. Low rain chances continue across \nthe Coastal Plains through the weekend into early next week.\n\nOne other concern to be monitored later in the week will be slightly \nhigher than normal tides and/or an increased rip current risk. \nModels show a long easterly fetch developing across the northern \ngulf waters by mid week which may help push water farther up the \nbeaches. At this time, minor coastal flooding is not anticipated but \nwill continue to be monitored.",
